WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:05.204
Captain Farragut was a good seaman, worthy of the fine ship that he commanded.

2
00:00:05.206 --> 00:00:11.293
He believed in the gigantic narwhal and would not let anyone onboard question its existence.

3
00:00:11.294 --> 00:00:15.293
The creature was real, and he had sworn to rid the seas of it.

4
00:00:15.294 --> 00:00:23.261
Either Captain Farragut would kill the narwhal, or the narwhal would kill Captain Farragut. There was no third option.

5
00:00:23.262 --> 00:00:27.436
The officers onboard the Abraham Lincoln shared their captain's opinion.

6
00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:32.348
They constantly discussed the narwhal, calculating the chances of meeting it.

7
00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:39.428
As for the crew, they wanted nothing more than to hunt down the sea unicorn, harpoon it, and hoist it onboard.

8
00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:48.166
To add to the excitement, Captain Farragut offered a reward of two thousand dollars to the first man who spotted the creature.

9
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:58.404
All eyes—including mine—were on the sea. Only one person onboard was indifferent to the chase and the reward, and that was Conseil.

10
00:00:58.405 --> 00:01:03.546
Captain Farragut had provided his ship with everything necessary to catch the gigantic narwhal.

11
00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:06.412
No whaling ship had ever been better equipped.

12
00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:11.983
But the Abraham Lincoln 's best asset was Ned Land, the king of

13
00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:15.229
harpooners. Ned had been born in Canada forty years

14
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.839
ago. Quick, cool, and cunning, he had no equal with the

15
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.483
harpoon. Originally from Quebec, the French-speaking part of Canada, Ned took a liking to

16
00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:32.888
me. Little by little, this quiet man and I got to know each other quite well. I loved to hear him tell of his adventures on the

17
00:01:32.889 --> 00:01:41.635
ocean. So what was his opinion on the question of the marine monster? I suspected that he did not believe in the sea

18
00:01:41.636 --> 00:01:48.618
unicorn. By the end of June, we had crossed the Atlantic and would soon reach Cape Horn at the southern tip of South

19
00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:54.698
America. Sitting on the deck with Ned, I led the conversation around to the topic of the gigantic narwhal.

20
00:01:54.699 --> 00:01:58.293
I examined the chances of success for our mission.

21
00:01:58.294 --> 00:02:05.865
"But you don't believe in the existence of this creature, do you?" I said. "Is there any particular reason for that?"

22
00:02:05.866 --> 00:02:12.611
The harpooner stared at me for a few moments before answering. "Perhaps there is, Professor Aronnax."

23
00:02:12.612 --> 00:02:21.341
"But, Ned, you're a whaler by profession, familiar with all the great marine mammals. You should be the last person to doubt its existence."

24
00:02:21.342 --> 00:02:28.349
"As a whaler, I've followed many large sea creatures and harpooned a great number of them," he said.

25
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:36.746
"But no matter how strong they were, neither their tails nor their tusks could have even scratched the iron plates of a steamship."

26
00:02:36.747 --> 00:02:42.405
I pointed out that there were reports of ships' hulls being pierced by the tusks of narwhals.

27
00:02:42.406 --> 00:02:48.914
"Wooden ships," Ned said carefully. "That might be possible, though I've never seen it happen.

28
00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:57.230
So until I have further proof, I refuse to believe that whales or sea unicorns could ever produce the effect you describe."

29
00:02:57.231 --> 00:03:01.897
I told him my theory that the creature might have come from unknown depths in the sea.

30
00:03:01.898 --> 00:03:07.817
Using mathematical equations, I explained how water pressure increases as water gets deeper.

31
00:03:07.818 --> 00:03:15.055
"Its body would have to be strong enough to resist water pressure equal to tens of millions of pounds."

32
00:03:15.056 --> 00:03:22.476
"Then this creature must be made of iron plates that are eight inches thick, like an armored warship!" exclaimed Ned.

33
00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:29.380
"Think what destruction such a creature would cause if it hurled itself against a ship's hull at the speed of an express train."

34
00:03:29.381 --> 00:03:36.555
"Yes, well . . . perhaps," Ned replied, shaken by these thoughts but not yet willing to give in.

35
00:03:36.556 --> 00:03:41.031
"So have I convinced you that such a creature exists?" I asked.

36
00:03:41.032 --> 00:03:48.285
"You've convinced me of one thing: that if this animal lives at the bottom of the sea, it must be as strong as you say."

37
00:03:48.286 --> 00:03:53.277
"But if it doesn't exist, how do you explain what happened to the Scotia

38
00:03:53.278 --> 00:04:00.436
?" "It . . ." Ned hesitated and then blurted out, "I explain it by pronouncing my conviction that it just couldn't be

39
00:04:00.437 --> 00:04:07.015
true!" I stopped trying to convince Ned. Our ship sailed around Cape Horn and on to the

40
00:04:07.016 --> 00:04:12.682
Pacific. By the end of July we had reached the northern Pacific, where the creature was last

41
00:04:12.683 --> 00:04:18.841
reported. The entire ship's crew was full of nervous excitement. They could not eat; they could not

42
00:04:18.842 --> 00:04:22.174
sleep. Twenty times a day someone thought he spotted our

43
00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:27.245
prey. For three more months the Abraham Lincoln sailed the waters of the northern

44
00:04:27.246 --> 00:04:35.904
Pacific. The days seemed to last forever as we explored every part of the ocean between Japan and North America. We found

45
00:04:35.905 --> 00:04:42.222
nothing. No ship's crew had ever worked harder; the men did everything they could to find the gigantic

46
00:04:42.223 --> 00:04:47.301
narwhal. But now the crew members were calling themselves fools for believing in such a

47
00:04:47.302 --> 00:04:53.911
creature. Every man wanted to catch up on his eating and sleeping, to make up for the time he had so stupidly

48
00:04:53.912 --> 00:04:58.063
sacrificed. There was only one thing left to do: go

49
00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:03.397
home. The men took this demand to Captain Farragut, but he refused to abandon the

50
00:05:03.398 --> 00:05:11.048
search. I won't say that they threatened to mutiny, but after their appeal to Farragut, they didn't perform their duties as well as they could

51
00:05:11.049 --> 00:05:19.119
have. At last Captain Farragut said that if the creature did not appear within three days, the pilot would turn the ship's wheel toward

52
00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:26.770
home. Captain Farragut made this promise on November 2, and it rallied the crew. Everyone watched the ocean with renewed attention.

53
00:05:26.771 --> 00:05:32.199
Two days passed during which a thousand schemes were tried to attract the creature.

54
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:38.357
Large quantities of bacon were trailed behind the ship, but that only succeeded in drawing sharks.

55
00:05:38.358 --> 00:05:43.278
When the ship stopped, men went out in small boats to continue their search.

56
00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:49.429
But November 4 arrived, and still the creature had not made an appearance.

57
00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.183
The deadline would expire at noon the next day.

58
00:05:53.184 --> 00:06:01.413
Night was approaching. Large clouds veiled the face of the moon. The sea rocked gently under the stern of the vessel.

59
00:06:01.414 --> 00:06:08.413
At that moment I was leaning over the railing. Conseil, standing near me, was looking straight ahead.

60
00:06:08.414 --> 00:06:13.580
The crew, perched in the rigging, was scanning the horizon in the growing darkness.

61
00:06:13.581 --> 00:06:19.651
"Come, Conseil," I said. "This is your last chance to earn the two thousand dollars."

62
00:06:19.652 --> 00:06:24.397
"If you will permit me to say this, I never counted on getting the prize."

63
00:06:24.398 --> 00:06:32.231
"You were right, Conseil," I admitted. "This was a foolish affair after all. We should have returned to France months ago."

64
00:06:32.232 --> 00:06:37.548
"You would be in your little apartment and in the museum," replied Conseil.

65
00:06:37.549 --> 00:06:40.830
"I would have already classified all of your fossils.

66
00:06:40.831 --> 00:06:47.143
And the wild swine would have been drawing crowds to its cage at the zoo in the botanical gardens."

67
00:06:47.144 --> 00:06:50.389
"You're right. We'll be laughed at for this adventure."

68
00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.429
"That's for sure," Conseil said quietly.

69
00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:59.961
"I think people will make fun of you. And I hope you don't mind my saying so, Master, but you deserve it."

70
00:06:59.962 --> 00:07:00.985
"What!"

71
00:07:00.986 --> 00:07:05.023
"You're a very smart man, so you shouldn't expose yourself to—"

72
00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:09.697
Just then someone started shouting. It was Ned Land.

73
00:07:09.698 --> 00:07:16.944
"Look! Over there!" cried Ned. "It's the very thing we've been searching for!"

